🚽 Test case: #206 (3e) -- an array field's trailing padding toward a
🚽 wider-aligned neighbor, the layout claim `maxxing()` on the whole
🚽 struct alone can't fully verify (a struct that packed `n` right after
🚽 `a` with no interior padding, then padded only the *total* to the same
🚽 final size, would report the same maxxing() result -- matching #206's
🚽 own "layout correctness is testable -- so test it" principle and the
🚽 read-back-without-corruption pattern struct_layout_padding.brainrot
🚽 (#242) already established for scalar fields).
🚽
🚽 `yap a[3]` is 3 bytes at align 1 -> offset 0. `rizz n` is 4 bytes at
🚽 align 4 -> offset 4 (1 byte of padding after a[2], not offset 3).
🚽 Total: 8 (already 4-aligned). Writing a[2] LAST (after n) makes a
🚽 regression that put n at offset 3 instead of 4 visible: n's 4-byte
🚽 write at the wrong offset would overlap a[2] and get silently
🚽 clobbered by a[2]'s own later write.
gang Padded {
    yap a[3];
    rizz n;
};

skibidi main {
    gang Padded p;
    p.n = 1234567;
    p.a[0] = 'r';
    p.a[1] = 'i';
    p.a[2] = 'z';
    yapping("%c%c%c %d", p.a[0], p.a[1], p.a[2], p.n);
    yapping("%lu", maxxing(p));
}
